Left Panel: X-ray spectral index evolution $ eta$ (where $F_{
u}\propto
u^{- eta}$ of all LFBOTs for which this parameter can be observationally constrained. Horizontal dotted lines: expected $ eta\approx 1$ value for synchrotron-powered X-rays above the cooling frequency when $F_{
u}\propto
u^{-p/2}$ and $p\approx 2$ (upper line), and X-rays below the cooling frequency ($F_{
u}\propto
u^{-(p-1)/2}$ and $p\approx 2$, lower line). The soft X-ray spectrum of a free-free cooling multi-temperature plasma also shows $ eta\approx 0.5$ (Govreen-Segal et al. 28, their Appendix B; Wasserman et al. 81), consistent with LFBOTs. Instead, a single-temperature thermal bremsstrahlung spectrum with temperature above $10$ keV would manifest as a $ eta\approx 0$ spectrum in this plot. Right Panel: resulting distribution for the LFBOT population (yellow shade) vs. GRB afterglows (grey shade). As a population, LFBOTs show harder X-ray emission than GRBs afterglows, which are known to be powered by the relativistic shock interaction with the medium. References: ATs 2018cow [50, 55]; 2020mrf [82]; 2020xnd [6]; 2022tsd (this work); 2023fhn (this work); 2024wpp [57]; 2024qfm (this work). GRBs: distribution of the average photon-counting mode spectra from the updated GRB X-ray catalog derived as in [21].
